WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:06.069
It's National Day in Taiwan.

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:11.690
Outside the presidential palace in Taipei,
this is more than a parade.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:16.590
It's a show of defiance – against an existential
threat.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.180
China's military has just mounted its biggest
show of force in a generation.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.619
A test run for a blockade or even invasion
of Taiwan.

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:30.730
A place China says is its own.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:36.140
Taiwan's president says NO – and her people
must be ready to fight.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:52.440
"We must defend our national sovereignty and
our free and democratic way of life.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.440
On this point, we have no room for compromise.”

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.390
A thousand miles away, in The Great Hall of
the People in Beijing.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:02.130
Xi Jinping is making Chinese Communist Party
history.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.570
With a third term at the top.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.680
And making his promise – and his threat.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.730
That one way or the other, Beijing will take
control of Taiwan.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:20.229
"We will strive for the prospect of a peaceful
reunification with the greatest sincerity

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:30.060
and greatest efforts, but will never commit
to abandoning the use of force."

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.820
His words echo across the Taiwan Strait

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:40.610
The Chinese government is adamant that it
wants to as it puts it "reunify" with Taiwan.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:47.710
And yet a growing majority of people in Taiwan
are equally adamant that they don't want that.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:54.250
Now that simple but profound tension looks
to be reaching breaking point.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:02:01.200
The US is preparing for conflict in this strategically
critical region… with Joe Biden saying America

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.829
would come to Taiwan's defence.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:06.600
And that would mean a very big war.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.960
"The escalation would almost be immediate.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.690
A general war involving at least three or
four, four or five countries, including the

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.550
three largest economies in the world.”

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:23.920
In a time already shaken by conflict in Europe,
this would plunge the world into a crisis

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.040
of historic proportions.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.810
In this video we find out how we got into
this dangerous situation.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.280
And how we might prevent it getting even worse.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.769
In part 1, we go back in time to uncover the
roots of the crisis.

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:46.140
How a diplomatic breakthrough kept the peace
for decades - but left a legacy of anger and

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.920
betrayal.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:55.909
"Everybody, all three angles in this triangle
are permeated with distrust.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:03:02.080
That is a consequence of the process we have
used to get to where we are today.”

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.030
In part 2, tensions begin to boil.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.760
"They fired missiles to the waters near Taiwan.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.319
They conducted very large-scale air and sea
exercises.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.019
They conducted cyber attacks against Taiwan.

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:21.720
The Chinese seems to be preparing for a war
against Taiwan”

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:28.900
And in part three we project the dynamics
in China, the US and Taiwan into the future.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.550
To see where this dispute is heading.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:37.920
And to find out what, if anything, can stop
it breaking out into war.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.720
"I think the danger is greater today than
it has ever been before.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:50.640
The US and China are in this spiral of threat
and counter threat, and counter-counter threat,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.351
and Taiwan is caught in the middle.”

00:03:53.351 --> 00:03:58.890
"We in mainland China believe that Taiwan
is part of China and we have to reunify with

00:03:58.890 --> 00:03:59.890
Taiwan.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:06.590
The only question is through what means: whether
they will be peaceful or whether we have to

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:07.590
use force.”

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:16.320
"I think the key thing is for Beijing to recognize
that if you go to war to seize Taiwan, you

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.320
lose.”

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.229
But let's begin by stepping back in time for
a moment.

00:04:20.229 --> 00:04:25.230
Because to really understand what's happening
now, you have to dig into to ANOTHER time

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.750
in history when the geopolitics of the world
were shifting…

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:34.940
And just one thing: If you know your history
really well, go ahead and jump to part 2.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:41.740
But I'd suggest you stick around for some
pretty fascinating details that show how diplomacy

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:50.419
can go right – and very wrong – at the
same time.

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:56.560
It's February 1972… and Air Force One touches
down in Beijing on a trip that would change

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.560
the world.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:04.530
US President Richard Nixon, breaking the ice
of the Cold War to seek friendship with "Red

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.760
China.”

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.139
Before this visit they had NO relationship
at all.

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:10.770
Now that was to change.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:23.389
"To the hope of our children, that peace and
harmony can be the legacy of our generation

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.389
to theirs."

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:29.130
The trip was a huge gamble but Nixon and his
national security advisor Henry Kissinger

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.740
had good reason for it.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:36.860
It was all about the battle for dominance
in a divided world – and exploiting tensions

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:42.300
between China's leader Mao Zedong and the
much bigger enemy: the USSR.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:49.009
"They were seeing that the - what they had
long believed to be a kind of unbreakable

00:05:49.009 --> 00:05:56.440
bond of socialist brotherhood between the
Soviet Union and China was actually not unbreakable

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.340
and they wanted to help break it.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:05.240
"What impressed me is that this is a kind
of grand chessboard movement.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:11.599
China, although at that time was a poor nation,
was still a very important chip, at this step

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:12.599
between the the two camps at loggerheads.

00:06:12.599 --> 00:06:13.599
So China is not as important as United States.

00:06:13.599 --> 00:06:14.599
China was not as important as the United States,
and China wasn't as important as Soviet Union.

00:06:14.599 --> 00:06:17.449
But if you put China on each side, then the
balance would change.

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:22.090
And Richard Nixon, of course, saw this, Kissinger
saw this.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.770
And so they - they did something.

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.710
But at the same time, the Chinese government
also saw this opportunity.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:31.840
"Mao had never really trusted the Communist
Party of the Soviet Union.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:38.440
By the mid 1960s they were actually having
military clashes on the border between China

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:39.440
and Russia.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:49.280
So China needed to find someone to provide
a little bit of counterweight to the Soviet

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.310
Union.”

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:56.330
So when Nixon and Mao finally met, both had
a major prize to play for.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:02.389
But before the meeting could actually happen
– they had to solve a massive problem.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:06.460
What to do about Taiwan.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:11.319
Decades earlier, the US had forged an alliance
with Chiang Kai-shek – the leader of the

00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:14.699
losing side in China's civil war.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:18.220
Chiang had fled the mainland for Taiwan in
1949…

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:25.370
And he claimed he was China's legitimate leader
- of what he called the "Republic of China.”

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:28.490
The American alliance had been crucial for
Taiwan ever since.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:33.010
But now, it was going to be sacrificed to
build relations with Beijing.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:37.490
This would be a diplomatic bombshell – and
a political one too.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:44.160
Just think about it: an American president,
dropping a longstanding CAPITALIST ally in

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.970
order to make friends with a Communist enemy
– in the middle of the Cold War!

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:55.500
Nixon had to prepare the ground incredibly
carefully - it had to work.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:01.719
Henry Kissinger, Nixon's trusted national
security advisor, was the man for the mission

00:08:01.719 --> 00:08:06.530
- he took a secret trip to Beijing to find
a solution.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:14.860
"What Kissinger needed was some kind of a
formula for talking about Taiwan that would

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:22.819
survive the scrutiny of American politicians,
many of whom strongly believed that the US

00:08:22.819 --> 00:08:33.019
should not throw over an anti-communist ally
in favor of creating a relationship with a

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:34.019
communist country.”

00:08:34.019 --> 00:08:39.450
Kissinger's sparring partner was Zhou Enlai,
prime minister and Chairman Mao's powerful

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.390
number 2.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:47.390
We don't have footage from the first secret
set of talks in July 1971 – but we do have

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:50.249
the official US transcripts.

00:08:50.249 --> 00:08:52.810
Taiwan was absolutely front and centre.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:58.880
Zhou set out his demands… expressing what
was an article of faith for the People's Republic

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.130
of China – the "One China Principle.”

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:05.839
"In recognizing China the US must do so unreservedly.

00:09:05.839 --> 00:09:12.580
It must recognize the PRC as the sole legitimate
government of China…

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.120
Taiwan is a Chinese province…

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:17.529
an inalienable part of Chinese territory.”

00:09:17.529 --> 00:09:25.459
"From their point of view, any territory that
was part of the Qing dynasty at its height

00:09:25.459 --> 00:09:29.960
is part of China and should be in the People's
Republic of China today.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:34.340
So the one China principle is: there is only
one China.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:40.740
Everything that was part of the Qing at its
largest extent is part of that China and the

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.990
capital of that China is Beijing.

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:49.250
And the legal government of that China is
the People's Republic of China.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:55.720
But over in Taiwan, Chiang Kai-shek ALSO claimed
to be the rightful leader of "One China”.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.209
A China not led by communists, but by nationalists.

00:10:00.209 --> 00:10:08.060
So Kissinger had to find a formula to satisfy
Beijing without completely enraging both Taiwan

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.899
and its American supporters.

00:10:09.899 --> 00:10:20.370
"So the fig leaf that they came up with was
for the US side to say: look, we get it.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:26.390
We just recognize the claim held by both sides
of the Taiwan strait that Taiwan is part of

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:31.580
China and all we say, all we want is peace
and stability.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:36.080
Solve the problem however you choose between
the two of you.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:37.800
But solve it peacefully.”

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:44.269
This fig leaf meant that Kissinger was establishing
a version of "One China” that was very different

00:10:44.269 --> 00:10:45.269
from Beijing's.

00:10:45.269 --> 00:10:51.630
At the same time, Kissinger gave Zhou Enlai
a hint that the Chinese would get their way

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:52.959
in the long run.

00:10:52.959 --> 00:10:58.889
"One's prediction would have to be that the
political evolution is likely to be in the

00:10:58.889 --> 00:11:03.420
direction which Prime Minister Zhou Enlai
indicated to me.”

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:09.780
But Kissinger's hint was not in tune with
the political reality back home in the US.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:18.399
"So basically Kissinger told Zhou Enlai 'Don't
worry about it, you'll get what you want,'

00:11:18.399 --> 00:11:24.420
knowing that other people in the United States
government would see to it that it was not

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:25.760
that easy.”

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:31.579
Still, Beijing went along with it and Nixon's
visit the following year produced the decisive

00:11:31.579 --> 00:11:37.250
steps towards establishing full relations….
which Jimmy Carter and Deng Xiaoping would

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:41.279
eventually formalise in 1979.

00:11:41.279 --> 00:11:46.430
This process left Taiwan looking very lonely
on the world stage.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.550
It lost its seat at the United Nations.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:56.149
And many other countries followed the US lead
in switching recognition from Taipei to Beijing.

00:11:56.149 --> 00:11:58.270
Some had already done so.

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:04.950
But that fig-leaf that Kissinger had produced
to get Zhou's acceptance was also the beginning

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:10.260
of a blurred, AMBIGUOUS US position towards
Taiwan and China…

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:14.520
with different parts of the US government
pulling in different directions.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:21.340
"US officials had to go back to Washington
and tell Congress, you know: we are going

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:27.690
to recognize the People's Republic of China
and we are going to end our mutual defense

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:36.690
treaty with Taiwan - And entirely predictably,
those American politicians, especially again

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:44.880
in Congress, said: hell no, you're not abandoning
Taiwan, we're not letting that happen.”

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:50.561
So Taiwan's supporters in Congress came up
with a law to soften the blow: The Taiwan

00:12:50.561 --> 00:12:51.700
Relations Act.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:58.750
"So they wrote legislation that requires the
US to treat Taiwan as if it were a country

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:07.829
that we recognized, and to continue to offer
Taiwan the capability to defend itself.”

00:13:07.829 --> 00:13:12.480
Now America's commitments in the Taiwan Relations
Act are very carefully defined…

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:18.300
They DON'T go as far as an alliance - that
would clearly be a breach of the pledges to

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.760
Beijing.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:25.040
Instead it walks a kind of blurry line that's
become central to America's position on Taiwan

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.810
ever since – known as 'strategic ambiguity.'

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:35.440
"By saying to Taiwan: you know, you don't
know for sure that we would support you if

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:38.600
you provoked Beijing to attack you.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:45.279
And by saying to Beijing: you don't know for
sure that we won't go to war against you if

00:13:45.279 --> 00:13:49.860
you attack Taiwan without provocation.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:58.950
So both sides are uncertain what the US response
to their own actions might be.

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:01.949
That's strategic ambiguity.”

00:14:01.949 --> 00:14:06.960
Despite all those hedges, the Taiwan Relations
Act's commitments went too far for Beijing

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:11.210
– which saw it as contradicting the deal
they had just agreed.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:18.120
"Between 79 and 82 Beijing was complaining
constantly about US arms sales to Taiwan,

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:21.759
like 'we thought we were getting Taiwan back.'”

00:14:21.759 --> 00:14:26.329
By this time Ronald Reagan was in the White
House… and he looked for new ways to keep

00:14:26.329 --> 00:14:32.089
all sides happy – with a new Communiqué
to satisfy the Chinese leadership.

00:14:32.089 --> 00:14:39.459
"In response to Beijing's complaints about
arms sales, the Reagan Administration said:

00:14:39.459 --> 00:14:48.820
look, as long as things stay peaceful, we
can reduce the amount and the lethality of

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:52.920
the arms we sell to Taiwan gradually over
time.”

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.920
Reagan tried to balance this out with a set
of promises not to abandon Taiwan any further

00:14:57.920 --> 00:14:58.920
– called the "six assurances.”

00:14:58.920 --> 00:14:59.920
"Then Reagan sent an official to Taipei to
say: look, you know, this doesn't mean that

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:00.920
we're giving up on you, this doesn't mean
that we're going to negotiate with Beijing

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:01.920
over the future of Taiwan.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:02.920
That's the six assurances.”

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.920
This was quite a bit of diplomatic gymnastics
by any standards.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.060
It did seem to settle things down for the
time being.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:12.889
But in the long run, it couldn't stop PROBLEMS
with trust mounting up.

00:15:12.889 --> 00:15:20.000
"If they agree that Taiwan is a part of China,
so why would they keep sending more and more

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:21.380
arms to Taiwan?

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:35.899
This kind of internal conflict or seemingly
discrepancy is actually a purposeful collaboration

00:15:35.899 --> 00:15:40.930
between the Congress and the White House.”

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:46.430
"Washington's rejoinder is: you guys are not
acting peaceful.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:51.860
And while the Taiwanese were happy to receive
the six assurances, they also have this permanent

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:58.509
concern that you know - eventually the US
is going to decide that protecting Taiwan

00:15:58.509 --> 00:16:03.449
is not worth it and they're going to wake
up one morning and find that they're on their

00:16:03.449 --> 00:16:05.110
own.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:13.540
So everybody, all three angles in this triangle
are permeated with distrust.

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:21.130
That is a consequence of the process we have
used to get to where we are today.”

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:27.029
And at the heart of all this: a profound mismatch
in perceptions of what the US and China even

00:16:27.029 --> 00:16:30.740
agreed all those decades ago.

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:34.510
Leaving unfinished business at the core of
their relationship.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:40.410
And sure enough, all of these contradictions
and senses of betrayal and distrust are now

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:52.050
bursting out into the open – into a very
different world.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:55.740
After nightfall in southern Taiwan.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.800
The Taiwanese army is holding live-fire exercises.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:03.680
Testing its strength to resist an invasion
by China.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:08.330
And determined to show that it's ready for
the fight.

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:14.360
"We will prepare for war but will not seek
war.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:17.260
We will fight but not seek conflicts.”

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.510
But conflict may be coming.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:23.449
Chinese military pressure has reached new
levels.

00:17:23.449 --> 00:17:31.360
Massive exercises in 2022 were seen as a partial
test-run for a blockade, or even invasion.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:35.020
Taiwan's foreign minister, Joseph Wu, condemned
the drills.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:39.210
"They fired missiles to the waters near Taiwan.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:43.760
They conducted very large-scale air and sea
exercises.

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:46.500
They conducted cyber attacks against Taiwan.

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:50.419
They conducted disinformation campaign against
Taiwan.

00:17:50.419 --> 00:17:51.960
So put it all together.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:56.360
This is what they want to do to Taiwan when
they want to invade Taiwan.”

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:02.299
The exercises broke with established practice,
crossing the so-called "Median Line” midway

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:04.641
between Taiwan and the mainland.

00:18:04.641 --> 00:18:08.650
"The median line of the Taiwan Strait has
been there for decades, safeguarding peace

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.730
and stability over the Taiwan Strait.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:16.789
And without that tacit agreement, the two
militaries are going to come very close to

00:18:16.789 --> 00:18:22.090
each other, and the Chinese are coming so
close that it can press the depths of our

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:25.550
air defense and it can create a very dangerous
situation.”

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:30.770
But the Chinese say they're just responding
to AMERICAN provocations.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:40.220
August 2022 – Nancy Pelosi lands in Taipei
for a visit that's set the world on edge.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:44.659
She's Speaker of the House of Representatives
– the most senior American politician to

00:18:44.659 --> 00:18:48.010
set foot in Taiwan in a generation.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:54.430
She tells her host, President Tsai Ing-wen:
she's come to support a democracy under threat.

00:18:54.430 --> 00:19:01.330
"We will not abandon our commitment to Taiwan
and we are proud of our enduring friendship…”

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:07.000
Beijing responded with outrage, insisting
the trip was a breach of US "One China”

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.000
policy.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.450
"For days China has through various channels
at multiple levels repeatedly expressed to

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:15.120
the United States China's stern position of
staunchly opposing Pelosi's Taiwan visit,

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:23.679
but the US and those Taiwan separatist forces
seem not to have heard.”

00:19:23.679 --> 00:19:28.730
And those massive military exercises started
within hours of Pelosi's departure.

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:32.540
The message: America made us do this.

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:39.960
"Honest answer is, if Nancy Pelosi didn't
make the visit, we could not have the chance

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:43.210
to have such a grand-scale exercise.

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:50.870
So it is the United States that has provoked
China and to make China respond.

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:57.409
We just let the world know that we would not
tolerate this kind of behavior and we - PLA

00:19:57.409 --> 00:20:00.830
- would stand ready to even use military actions.”

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:06.179
But the Taiwanese say the Pelosi visit really
wasn't such a big deal.

00:20:06.179 --> 00:20:11.510
"If they can find Speaker Pelosi's visit with
Taiwan as an excuse to launch such a large

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:18.620
military scale exercise, I think they can
find many more excuses.

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:25.270
The Chinese military threat against Taiwan
will not stop Taiwan from making more friends.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:32.150
And at the same time, it will not stop international
friends to come to Taiwan and show their support

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:33.150
to us.”

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:38.520
Sure enough, western lawmakers have been streaming
to Taiwan, including two delegations from

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:41.770
Germany soon after Pelosi's visit.

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:46.770
One participant told us that the trips were
fully in line with Germany's version of 'One

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:47.770
China.'

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:51.320
"It's no breach of the policy we have for
many, many years.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:57.490
It would be a change if we would hesitate
visiting Taiwan in these times.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:03.809
And that's that would be an important negative
sign for Taiwan if we wouldn't go there anymore,

00:21:03.809 --> 00:21:08.990
in these times where China is threatening
Taiwan.”

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:15.020
Halfway around the world, the war in Ukraine
is also a major factor.

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:19.950
Russia's invasion is increasing the sense
among Western politicians that they need to

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:21.960
look out for Taiwan.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:27.980
Germany's foreign minister Annalena Baerbock
draws a direct connection between Russia's

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:31.220
invasion and the Chinese threat to Taiwan.

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:36.950
"This fundamental breach of international
law by Russia against Ukraine cannot be tolerated

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:43.090
because otherwise it would be an invitation
to other countries like China to also invade

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:44.090
Taiwan.”

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:49.130
"Russia's invasion of Ukraine has been a giant
wake-up call to Taiwan.

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:53.700
Not just for political leaders or the military
but for ordinary people too.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:22:02.890
They're confronting the reality — if it
can happen there, it could happen here too.”

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:09.230
NGOs have been holding training sessions across
Taiwan to try to get the public better prepared.

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:14.990
"The biggest lesson from Ukraine is that it's
a sharp reminder that peace is so fragile.

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:18.120
It's a sharp reminder that our world can change
overnight.”

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:24.460
Here in the city of Taichung, people are learning
basic skills they might need in a conflict.

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:26.760
"We're training civilians in crisis response.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:32.130
So that means teaching communities how to
do first aid, how to treat severe trauma,

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:36.309
how to do search and rescue, how to do emergency
communication when our phones don't work,

00:22:36.309 --> 00:22:39.400
how to manage shelters, essentially, how to
keep communities running, how to care after

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:40.400
your loved ones and your neighbors.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:41.400
That's what we're after.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.400
It helps prepare citizens against crises from
man-made - whether manmade or natural.”

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.980
People come to spend their Saturday afternoon
preparing for something they hope will never

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:45.980
happen.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:54.650
"With the Ukraine war, things kind of changed,
I think there is a bit more awareness and

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:58.990
sensitivity towards how vulnerable our situation
can be.”

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:04.720
"I think it really helps on creating these
scenarios that help us think, ok - we are

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:11.039
actually not in a place that is safe and we
need to know how to protect ourselves when

00:23:11.039 --> 00:23:12.039
things happen.”

00:23:12.039 --> 00:23:20.730
This reflects Taiwan's growing sense of itself
as distinct from China.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:25.750
Surveys show that a large majority of the
population now identifies as Taiwanese, rather

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:26.750
than Chinese.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:32.420
And only a tiny minority want to 'reunify'
with China.

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:33.770
"We elect our own president.

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:34.770
We have a constitution.

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.340
We have a standing army.

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:37.340
We have citizens.

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:38.480
We have the right to vote.

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:41.210
We know that Taiwan exists.”

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:45.870
And economically, Taiwan has become a global
force to be reckoned with.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:51.940
Its semiconductor industry is the most advanced
in the world – and critical to global supply

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:53.730
chains.

00:23:53.730 --> 00:24:00.090
The DPP party of president Tsai Ing-wen is
known for championing Taiwan's autonomy and

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:02.910
pushing back vigorously against Chinese pressure.

00:24:02.910 --> 00:24:10.540
"The situation around the Taiwan Strait is
tense and the threat is increasing day by

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:11.540
day.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:14.870
During this period, the troops of the national
army at all levels have stepped up their vigilance.”

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:20.030
Tsai announced a big hike in military spending
just after the Chinese exercises…

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:23.880
And her government says there will be more
to come…

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:29.049
The US is by far Taiwan's biggest supplier
of arms … and there's strong support in

00:24:29.049 --> 00:24:31.870
Congress to keep the weapons flowing.

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:40.789
"I think there is a strong bipartisan consensus
in seeing China as the pacing threat — economically,

00:24:40.789 --> 00:24:44.909
technologically, diplomatically and militarily.

00:24:44.909 --> 00:24:47.760
So I think that bipartisan consensus is there.

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.559
And it's very, very important.

00:24:50.559 --> 00:24:56.360
And it's certainly there with - in terms of
helping build up Taiwan's ability to defend

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:57.360
itself.”

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:03.720
And this only makes Beijing angrier… saying
it breaks with those deals made decades ago.

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:08.740
"US administrations have been violating such
kind of agreement by increasing arms sales

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:12.659
to Taiwan, sending more and more officials
to visit Taiwan.

00:25:12.659 --> 00:25:15.320
So this is clearly in violation of what they
said.

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:22.399
If they agree that Taiwan is a part of China,
so why would they keep selling more and more

00:25:22.399 --> 00:25:24.940
arms to Taiwan?”

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:30.600
But for all China's anger over the US arming
Taiwan, it's the massive growth in Beijing's

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:33.910
own military that is the real game changer.

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:41.130
"The People's Liberation Army at the moment
is undergoing a large-scale reform and modernisation

00:25:41.130 --> 00:25:46.190
process under the leadership and guidance
of President Xi Jinping.

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:52.929
Adding more advanced and more capabilities,
more modern capabilities to the PLA's inventory

00:25:52.929 --> 00:25:58.510
in the air, sea, land – and of course also
in space and cyber domains.”

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:03.740
And that process is pushing ahead on an accelerated
timetable.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:10.140
"We probably will become as strong as the
United States.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:20.760
You see officially in mid-century, but probably
we can speed up that a bit.”

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:26.559
The man pushing all this forward: Xi Jinping,
China's supreme leader.

00:26:26.559 --> 00:26:31.800
He insists that 'reunification' with Taiwan
is China's destiny.

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:35.860
And he makes no bones about it: he reserves
the right to invade.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:38.390
"Resolving the Taiwan issue is a matter for
Chinese people themselves, and must be resolved

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:39.390
by Chinese people alone.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:49.210
We will strive for the prospect of a peaceful
reunification with the greatest sincerity

00:26:49.210 --> 00:27:02.850
and greatest efforts, but will never commit
to abandoning the use of force.”

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:08.929
America's top diplomat Tony Blinken takes
this seriously - and says Xi might act sooner

00:27:08.929 --> 00:27:10.000
rather than later.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:13.669
"There has been a change in the approach from
Beijing toward Taiwan in recent years.

00:27:13.669 --> 00:27:22.360
And instead of sticking with the status quo
that was established in a positive way, a

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:30.280
fundamental decision that the status quo was
no longer acceptable, and that Beijing was

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:36.909
determined to pursue reunification on a much
faster timeline.”

00:27:36.909 --> 00:27:39.169
What does this mean for the US position?

00:27:39.169 --> 00:27:43.360
What would it do if Beijing pulled the trigger?

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:48.340
On several occasions now, Joe Biden has said:
it would fight.

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:52.890
"Are you willing to get involved militarily
to defend Taiwan, if it comes to that?”

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:53.890
"Yes.

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:56.490
That's the commitment we made.”

00:27:56.490 --> 00:28:00.590
"Here is the situation.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:06.809
We agreed with the One China policy, we signed
on to it, and all the attendant agreements

00:28:06.809 --> 00:28:07.809
made from there.

00:28:07.809 --> 00:28:15.159
But the idea that it can be taken by force
is just not appropriate.

00:28:15.159 --> 00:28:21.510
It will dislocate the entire region, and will
be another action similar to what happened

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:25.120
in Ukraine."

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:30.121
Biden and his team have repeatedly gone on
to equivocate over these statements, seeming

00:28:30.121 --> 00:28:36.250
to walk back from such clarity and return
to that position of ambiguity nurtured over

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:37.680
decades.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:40.560
But Beijing is drawing its own conclusions.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:46.070
"This is what we believe they are doing: They
are hollowing out this concept of one China,

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:52.020
because they still talk about it, but the
substance is being changed.

00:28:52.020 --> 00:29:01.220
With more such kinds of visits, with more
arms sales to Taiwan, with Joe Biden talking

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:05.919
four times about how the United States will
defend Taiwan militarily.

00:29:05.919 --> 00:29:08.850
The substance, we are afraid, is changing.”

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:13.780
"I think if you look at it from both sides,
from the Beijing angle and the Washington

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:20.120
angle, there is a case to be made that both
are simultaneously salami-slicing the traditional

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:22.049
notion of the one-China policy.

00:29:22.049 --> 00:29:30.450
So the Americans, when you start playing around
with the question of official level contact

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:36.580
between the authorities in Taiwan and those
of the US administration – the Chinese would

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:43.820
argue that this begins to bit-by-bit salami-slice
the whole notion of "one China" becoming "two

00:29:43.820 --> 00:29:44.820
China."

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:50.340
That gets turbocharged by the erosion of the
American traditional doctrine of strategic

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:57.570
ambiguity into strategic unambiguity, which
is where President Biden has left it most

00:29:57.570 --> 00:29:58.570
recently.

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:04.669
Mind you, on the other end, the Americans
would say that China has always known that

00:30:04.669 --> 00:30:08.700
America would be opposed to any threat of
the use of force.

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:16.550
And the deployment of military assets after
the Pelosi visit came after a series of efforts

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:23.070
by China in recent years to ramp up its own
military efforts in and around Taiwan anyway.

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:29.320
So the Americans would argue that China, too,
is salami-slicing one-China policy by beginning

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:33.399
to make more and more recourse to military
preparations to take the island.”

00:30:33.399 --> 00:30:38.820
There's a growing sense that we're approaching
a moment of truth…

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:48.559
So in part 3 of this video we're going to
find out – what if anything can prevent

00:30:48.559 --> 00:30:51.200
war?

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:56.470
Bali, Indonesia.

00:30:56.470 --> 00:31:02.200
The leaders of the 20 most powerful nations
have come to a tropical paradise… to talk

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:04.470
about a world in crisis.

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:10.160
This is the G20 summit – a group that's
meant to coordinate the global economy.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:16.250
But right now it's dominated by the geopolitical
struggles engulfing the world.

00:31:16.250 --> 00:31:22.170
The summit host, Indonesia's president Joko
Widodo – sums up the fears of leaders in

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:23.649
the region.

00:31:23.649 --> 00:31:30.380
"We should not divide the world in two parts.

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:37.370
We must not allow the world to fall into another
cold war.”

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:42.450
That message was aimed directly at the big
beasts at the gathering: Joe Biden and Xi

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:44.200
Jinping.

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:49.750
Here not just for the summit but for a hotly
awaited meeting – their first since Biden

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:53.080
became president.

00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:58.210
The two men put on smiles as they finally
came face to face… and with the cameras

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:03.549
still running, spoke of the need to get talking
again.

00:32:03.549 --> 00:32:11.840
"Since you assumed the presidency, we have
maintained communication via video conferences,

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:14.679
phone calls and letters.

00:32:14.679 --> 00:32:22.820
But none of them can really substitute for
face-to-face exchanges.”

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:30.240
"As the leaders of our two nations, we share
responsibility, in my view, to show that China

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:37.390
and the United States can manage our differences,
prevent competition from becoming anything

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:38.909
ever near conflict.”

00:32:38.909 --> 00:32:44.400
And if you're wondering just how dysfunctional
things have been between the US and China?

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:51.649
Consider this, from one of the leading defence
experts in Washington – someone close to

00:32:51.649 --> 00:32:52.649
the administration.

00:32:52.649 --> 00:32:57.179
"We have none of the risk reduction measures
in place that we had even with the Soviet

00:32:57.179 --> 00:32:59.010
Union at the height of the Cold War.

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:03.210
So there's no hotline where we know someone
will pick up at the other end.

00:33:03.210 --> 00:33:09.010
There are no risk reduction protocols that
we can use in a crisis to de-escalate if there's

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:11.230
some miscalculation.”

00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:14.120
A crisis could easily break out.

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:18.871
With China's growing assertiveness around
Taiwan – and the US presence in the region

00:33:18.871 --> 00:33:23.780
– the scope for dangerous encounters is
very significant.

00:33:23.780 --> 00:33:29.850
Australia's former prime minister and China
specialist Kevin Rudd wants the two sides

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:31.539
to make compromises.

00:33:31.539 --> 00:33:37.059
"It would mean that the Americans should not
move down the road further on the question

00:33:37.059 --> 00:33:42.549
of salami slicing the one-China policy and
the Chinese should establish a much wider

00:33:42.549 --> 00:33:48.919
margin of geographical separation between
their current military activities in eastern

00:33:48.919 --> 00:33:51.550
Fujian Province and the island of Taiwan itself.”

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:57.669
But even if Beijing and Washington can work
out some effective boundaries for now, the

00:33:57.669 --> 00:34:00.269
basic problem hasn't gone anywhere.

00:34:00.269 --> 00:34:09.310
"Whenever Taiwan is raised with Chinese officials,
they – it's a non-negotiable issue.

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:14.849
They it's a what they call a core interest
and not something that they're willing to

00:34:14.849 --> 00:34:16.349
compromise in any way on.”

00:34:16.349 --> 00:34:18.419
So looking ahead, what does that mean?

00:34:18.419 --> 00:34:26.600
"Stabilising the relationship for the short
to medium term, to reduce the risk of unplanned,

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:31.849
accidental crisis, conflict and war in the
relationship over Taiwan or other strategic

00:34:31.849 --> 00:34:38.679
red line issues - that's quite different from
the long-term question of China's preparations

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:46.690
for the 2030s, which is to have sufficient
military and economic and technological power

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:51.040
to wage war not by accident but by design,
to secure Taiwan.”

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:56.399
And when you look at all the players in this
– at China, at the US and at Taiwan in the

00:34:56.399 --> 00:35:02.310
middle – you get a daunting sense of all
three set on collision course.

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:07.369
So in this final part of the video, we're
going to look at the dynamics at play – and

00:35:07.369 --> 00:35:10.790
find out – how can they avoid conflict?

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:14.320
Let's start with China.

00:35:14.320 --> 00:35:19.930
Xi Jinping could now be in power for another
full decade – or even more.

00:35:19.930 --> 00:35:26.270
He's declared an historic overall mission:
the "rejuvenation” of the Chinese nation.

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:32.540
Xi's government set it all down in this paper
on Taiwan published just before the big Party

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:34.060
Congress.

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:39.190
It contains 28 mentions of "national rejuvenation.”

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:42.280
And how do you get national rejuvenation?

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:43.869
By reunifying with Taiwan.

00:35:43.869 --> 00:35:49.700
"Xi Jinping wants to be the guy in political
office who finally delivers Taiwan back to

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:50.700
Chinese sovereignty.”

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:56.140
"We in mainland China believe that Taiwan
is part of China and we have to reunify with

00:35:56.140 --> 00:35:57.140
Taiwan.

00:35:57.140 --> 00:36:04.349
The only question is through what means: whether
they will be peaceful or whether we have to

00:36:04.349 --> 00:36:05.349
use force.”

00:36:05.349 --> 00:36:10.680
"What's changing and therefore making it more
dangerous is that China has now begun to perfect

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:14.960
the military technologies to execute the mission.

00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:20.119
China now doubts America's political resolve
and military capability to resist.”

00:36:20.119 --> 00:36:25.960
The military drills we saw in part 2 reinforced
this picture.

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:31.579
Still, representative Chinese voices insist
that they are in no hurry to use force.

00:36:31.579 --> 00:36:37.920
"It is the last option, because, of course,
it is in our own best interest to have peaceful

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:43.770
reunification with Taiwan, which I believe
one day will be realised.”

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:51.040
"We in mainland China are not actually that
impatient to say that we have to have a timetable

00:36:51.040 --> 00:36:53.160
for reunification.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:37:00.089
You have never seen any announcements of Chinese
government for a timetable of reunification.”

00:37:00.089 --> 00:37:05.850
And yet – some are questioning whether Taiwan's
growing sense of self we saw in part 2 might

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:07.990
start to change that calculation.

00:37:07.990 --> 00:37:14.840
"In the past, we've thought about how Beijing
thinks that it has time in its favor and in

00:37:14.840 --> 00:37:20.530
its advantage – that it can wait for the
opportunities or the political developments

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:26.619
in Taiwan to change on the ground to suit
conditions that it sees as more favorable

00:37:26.619 --> 00:37:28.830
for peaceful reunification.

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:32.579
The trends that are happening in Taiwan at
the moment, politically speaking, demographically

00:37:32.579 --> 00:37:36.829
speaking, don't make that very likely in the
near term future.”

00:37:36.829 --> 00:37:43.800
And when you ask the Chinese side under what
circumstances they would choose force – the

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:47.020
response leaves almost anything possible.

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:49.760
Let's hear about that from Zhou Bo.

00:37:49.760 --> 00:37:55.310
He was a high-ranking officer in the PLA,
and is now a senior analyst at one of China's

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:57.480
top universities.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:38:00.640
That means: he's closely connected to those
in power.

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:07.589
"There are actually three situations in which
non-peaceful means would have to be used.

00:38:07.589 --> 00:38:14.190
One is the declaration of independence by
the separatists in Taiwan, which I believe

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:20.109
is not quite possible because they won't be
so stupid to suddenly declare independence

00:38:20.109 --> 00:38:23.440
to invite a military response from the mainland.”

00:38:23.440 --> 00:38:26.380
He's right – this scenario is unlikely.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:31.609
The Taiwanese government has consistently
gone out of its way to avoid any official

00:38:31.609 --> 00:38:33.050
declaration of independence.

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:41.710
"The second is major events that lead to the
separation of Taiwan from mainland China.

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:49.280
I personally believe this is a some kind of
situation, just like the Nancy Pelosi visit

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:55.050
to Taiwan that is basically about external
interferences.”

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:59.069
This scenario is wide open though.

00:38:59.069 --> 00:39:02.270
As Zhou points out, the Pelosi visit actually
happened.

00:39:02.270 --> 00:39:10.560
"The third situation is that if the mainland
believes that this kind of separation will

00:39:10.560 --> 00:39:17.109
last forever and all possibilities of peaceful
reunification are exhausted.”

00:39:17.109 --> 00:39:20.740
And this final scenario is equally vague.

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:23.680
China could declare it at any time.

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:30.220
And permeating the Chinese thinking in these
scenarios is the idea that just one thing

00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:34.369
stands in the way of China's destiny: America.

00:39:34.369 --> 00:39:42.089
"I think it's really important to remember
that the centrality of the Taiwan issue to

00:39:42.089 --> 00:39:47.050
the Chinese Communist Party is entirely a
decision that the Chinese Communist Party

00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:48.480
leadership has made, right?

00:39:48.480 --> 00:39:49.480
Why do they prioritize it?

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:57.830
I think they prioritize it because they believe
that the only reason Taiwan is still not in

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:03.120
their grasp is that the US is frustrating
their intentions.

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:14.630
So Taiwan becomes a kind of pawn that Beijing
is grappling over with the United States.”

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:20.160
And you sense increasing confidence in Beijing
that it CAN take that pawn.

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:22.310
"China is getting stronger.

00:40:22.310 --> 00:40:27.760
And because China is getting stronger and
because China very likely would surpass the

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:33.089
United States to become the largest economy
by the end of 2030.

00:40:33.089 --> 00:40:37.500
So this is a heavy blow to America's mentality.

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:47.230
We in the mainland are firm about our determination
to have reunification one day, and will continue

00:40:47.230 --> 00:40:54.560
to build our military to be better prepared
even for a worst-case scenario.”

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:58.710
And the White House is preparing for the worst-case
scenario too.

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:04.180
The latest National Security Strategy from
the president's team makes clear that it sees

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:09.319
China as the number 1 threat to America's
standing in the world.

00:41:09.319 --> 00:41:16.150
It says China has 'both the intent to reshape
the international order and, increasingly,

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:21.080
the economic, diplomatic, military, and technological
power to do it.'

00:41:21.080 --> 00:41:26.580
The US response to this is incredibly wide-ranging
– and specific.

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:33.490
In one move, for example, the Americans pretty
much cut China off from their highest-end

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:35.210
semiconductor technologies.

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:42.069
Not just the chips themselves, but any US-related
technology used to make them.

00:41:42.069 --> 00:41:50.840
This sounds pretty technical, but it's a momentous
severing of ties to slow down China's technological

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:52.000
development.

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:55.250
And the official behind the rule says there's
more to come.

00:41:55.250 --> 00:42:02.560
"We are going to continue to look at not just
semiconductors but other areas that the Chinese

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:06.530
are using to threaten the United States and
its allies.”

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:11.510
And specifically on Taiwan – the declared
US strategy is also firm.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:18.470
'We will uphold our commitments under the
Taiwan Relations Act to support Taiwan's self-defense

00:42:18.470 --> 00:42:25.040
and to maintain our capacity to resist any
resort to force or coercion against Taiwan.'

00:42:25.040 --> 00:42:32.339
There's also a growing sense of urgency among
leading defence analysts – a fear that the

00:42:32.339 --> 00:42:36.940
timelines they've been thinking of so far
are shrinking fast.

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:43.210
"We've been preparing for that, thinking that
maybe maybe that's something that - we could

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:44.550
see a crisis in the 2030s.

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:49.599
And so most of US defense planning is focused
on that time period.

00:42:49.599 --> 00:42:59.450
But what I think we've started to understand
is, maybe events could conspire to encourage

00:42:59.450 --> 00:43:02.670
Xi to act sooner.

00:43:02.670 --> 00:43:12.120
We have to be prepared to deter China not
just in ten, fifteen years, but in five years.”

00:43:12.120 --> 00:43:15.380
Allies in Europe are increasingly concerned
too.

00:43:15.380 --> 00:43:20.580
Germany's Chancellor Olaf Scholz – known
for his cautious approach to China – used

00:43:20.580 --> 00:43:26.690
his first visit there as chancellor to publicly
urge Beijing to safeguard stability.

00:43:26.690 --> 00:43:31.559
"Like the US and many other countries, we
have a one-China policy.

00:43:31.559 --> 00:43:40.440
At the same time I made clear that any change
in the status quo regarding Taiwan must only

00:43:40.440 --> 00:43:43.870
happen peacefully and by mutual consent.”

00:43:43.870 --> 00:43:48.770
And Scholz's deputy said this about the prospect
of a fight over Taiwan.

00:43:48.770 --> 00:43:52.200
"This will have a disastrous effect on the
whole world.

00:43:52.200 --> 00:43:58.150
We have seen that a regional conflict, like
the Russian war on Ukraine has brought the

00:43:58.150 --> 00:43:59.690
whole world into turbulence.

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:02.300
That would be far more catastrophic.

00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:04.440
So this is something we definitely have to
avoid.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:09.170
And I hope that China knows that the whole
world is watching.”

00:44:09.170 --> 00:44:14.980
And for America's friends in Asia, the prospect
of such a war on their doorstep is deeply

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:17.070
unnerving.

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:22.150
So the US is piling resources into the region,
trying to strengthen its position and get

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:24.260
its allies up to speed too…

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:32.290
The AUKUS alliance to supply Australia with
advanced nuclear submarines is a prime example.

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:37.589
The overriding goal: deterring China from
launching an attack.

00:44:37.589 --> 00:44:39.859
"Deterrence is the name of the game here.

00:44:39.859 --> 00:44:42.480
This is not a war that either side should
want to ever fight.

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:51.351
I think the key thing is for Beijing to recognize
that if you go to war to seize Taiwan, you

00:44:51.351 --> 00:44:52.351
lose.”

00:44:52.351 --> 00:44:58.819
But seen from China, all of this is an attempt
to obstruct its legitimate rise as a global

00:44:58.819 --> 00:44:59.819
power.

00:44:59.819 --> 00:45:04.490
A policy of containment – Cold-war style.

00:45:04.490 --> 00:45:13.380
"Clearly American intention is to contain
China while China is still not number one

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:14.859
power in the world.

00:45:14.859 --> 00:45:21.220
So under this kind of background comes to
this question: How useful would Taiwan be

00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:24.710
for the United States to contain China?

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:27.560
So this is my suspicion.

00:45:27.560 --> 00:45:32.880
Otherwise, why would the United States strengthen
its ties with Taiwan?”

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:41.480
And, as we saw earlier, it looks like US support
for Taiwan is only going to increase – one

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:46.329
of the few things that America's bitterly
divided political system can agree on.

00:45:46.329 --> 00:45:53.069
"Here is one thing you can say that will get
both sides of the aisle to support your proposal,

00:45:53.069 --> 00:45:56.760
and that is: this is against China.

00:45:56.760 --> 00:46:04.359
So a lot of what we see are US politicians
jumping on the bandwagon, which is, I think,

00:46:04.359 --> 00:46:11.460
really fundamentally an anti-China bandwagon,
but they have mistaken the anti-China bandwagon

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.829
for a pro-Taiwan bandwagon.”

00:46:13.829 --> 00:46:19.849
From this perspective, American support for
Taiwan becomes a paradox.

00:46:19.849 --> 00:46:25.650
At once essential for its survival…. and
yet potentially dragging it into the middle

00:46:25.650 --> 00:46:27.380
of a much larger fight.

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:34.569
"I think the danger is greater today than
it has ever been before, and I think the primary

00:46:34.569 --> 00:46:41.820
responsibility for that is in the deteriorating
relationship between the US and China, in

00:46:41.820 --> 00:46:51.730
which both sides are perceiving the other
as profoundly threatening and perceiving themselves

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:54.450
as behaving defensively.

00:46:54.450 --> 00:47:01.220
So the US and China are in this spiral of
threat and counter threat, and counter-counter

00:47:01.220 --> 00:47:05.140
threat, and Taiwan is caught in the middle.”

00:47:05.140 --> 00:47:10.319
And here in Taiwan itself, you can't escape
that feeling… especially in a place like

00:47:10.319 --> 00:47:13.810
Kinmen island, just off the Chinese coast.

00:47:13.810 --> 00:47:18.890
A place bristling with defences that have
been here for decades.

00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:25.990
"If you think of Taiwan as a potential front
line in a conflict between the US and China,

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:30.859
well then this really has to be the front
line of the front line.

00:47:30.859 --> 00:47:37.240
Here in Kinmen we look across the bay here
to the Chinese city of Xiamen, less than 5

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:41.030
km away across through the haze…

00:47:41.030 --> 00:47:43.260
This is China's doorstep.

00:47:43.260 --> 00:47:47.320
It feels very, very close.”

00:47:47.320 --> 00:47:53.859
And Kinmen has been directly targeted during
China's military exercises, with drones probing

00:47:53.859 --> 00:47:55.780
at guardposts.

00:47:55.780 --> 00:48:02.200
In this instance, Taiwanese soldiers were
reduced to repelling them with rocks.

00:48:02.200 --> 00:48:05.270
The incident exposed gaps in Taiwan's defences.

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:09.099
"The Chinese are trying to engage in cognitive
warfare.

00:48:09.099 --> 00:48:16.230
They want to shoot some scenes and to show
in their social media to show that the Taiwanese

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:18.849
soldiers are incompetent, things like that.”

00:48:18.849 --> 00:48:23.710
So what CAN Taiwan do in the face of such
a gigantic threat?

00:48:23.710 --> 00:48:29.180
"The buzzword when talking about defending
Taiwan is 'asymmetrical warfare.'

00:48:29.180 --> 00:48:35.150
Based on the idea that the Taiwanese side
will always be outnumbered and outspent by

00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:39.950
China — so how best to deal with that situation.”

00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:46.340
This is sometimes called a "porcupine strategy”
– to make Taiwan as prickly as possible.

00:48:46.340 --> 00:48:52.089
"Their job is to buy time in the event of
Chinese aggression so that the international

00:48:52.089 --> 00:48:54.540
community can respond and assist.

00:48:54.540 --> 00:49:03.109
So it's things like sea mines, anti-ship missiles,
air defenses, missile defenses, mobile systems,

00:49:03.109 --> 00:49:05.410
anything, and actually resistance.

00:49:05.410 --> 00:49:11.440
I mean, we've seen the power of the population
resisting in Ukraine.

00:49:11.440 --> 00:49:18.299
Well, Taiwan needs to organize itself to be
able to mobilize that kind of popular resistance.”

00:49:18.299 --> 00:49:24.580
This is where people like Enoch Wu come in,
training citizens in survival skills.

00:49:24.580 --> 00:49:29.110
He has a military background and has close
connections to Taiwan's government

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:33.299
"Our national survival is Taiwan's biggest
challenge of this generation.

00:49:33.299 --> 00:49:36.340
And so this threat has been around for a long
time.

00:49:36.340 --> 00:49:39.490
What's different is that it's escalating as
such a pace.

00:49:39.490 --> 00:49:44.600
It's important to train in a way that allows
our defense to be more resilient and more

00:49:44.600 --> 00:49:47.020
survivable and more credible.

00:49:47.020 --> 00:49:53.120
That means being trained to fight and operate
in a decentralized manner, in a dispersed

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:57.850
manner, so that these are all work that we
need to do because the PLA is continuing to

00:49:57.850 --> 00:50:00.680
invest in its capability and we need to keep
up.

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:06.860
Enoch Wu unapologetically rejects the formula
of 'one China' – while stopping short of

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:09.859
China's red line on formal 'independence.'

00:50:09.859 --> 00:50:11.910
"There is one China and there's one Taiwan.

00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:16.700
And I think for all intents and purposes,
everyone, the 23 million citizens that live

00:50:16.700 --> 00:50:19.540
in Taiwan, we understand that we are already
independent.

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:24.790
The fact that we can vote and decide our own
future, chart our own path, means that we're

00:50:24.790 --> 00:50:26.320
independent.”

00:50:26.320 --> 00:50:30.350
But others call for a more conciliatory tone
towards China.

00:50:30.350 --> 00:50:37.440
Eric Chu leads Taiwan's opposition Kuomintang
party or KMT – which ruled for most of Taiwan's

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:41.230
postwar history but is now in opposition.

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:44.880
He draws very different lessons from the Ukraine
war.

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:55.609
"We strongly encourage our government also
to create any channel of dialogue, any channel

00:50:55.609 --> 00:51:02.400
of positive - or any possibility to avoid
the accident or avoid the crisis of war.

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:03.400
We don't want to...

00:51:03.400 --> 00:51:04.809
We don't want to copy any trouble or any war
before.

00:51:04.809 --> 00:51:10.710
Just like whatever happened you see in Europe,
Ukraine.

00:51:10.710 --> 00:51:16.869
We strongly support the people of Ukraine
to defend their country.

00:51:16.869 --> 00:51:21.510
But I think Taiwan can learn from the Ukraine
war.

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:32.210
If we can maintain the channel of dialogue
between Taiwan and China or Beijing and Washington,

00:51:32.210 --> 00:51:35.780
I think the risk could be reduced.”

00:51:35.780 --> 00:51:42.430
Is there any prospect of some historic new
breakthrough on the level of the 1970s?

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:48.280
Something to finally solve the issues that
Kissinger and Zhou Enlai kicked down the road?

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:53.470
"I believe that some creativity needs to be
applied to this, both by the PRC, the United

00:51:53.470 --> 00:52:00.069
States and the Taiwanese to get back to a
political track somewhere on some formula

00:52:00.069 --> 00:52:05.010
for the future which might accommodate both
sides' long-term political aspirations.

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:07.380
Easy to say, hard to do.

00:52:07.380 --> 00:52:10.220
It certainly won't be "One China, two systems."

00:52:10.220 --> 00:52:15.200
One country, two systems - that was the deal
China struck when it took Hong Kong back from

00:52:15.200 --> 00:52:19.099
Britain.

00:52:19.099 --> 00:52:24.190
China's recent repression there has left the
idea with little credibility.

00:52:24.190 --> 00:52:29.119
"One China, three systems" – well, that's
a possibility.

00:52:29.119 --> 00:52:36.079
Are there permutations and combinations, combinations
of some greater Chinese confederation, which

00:52:36.079 --> 00:52:42.520
leaves Taiwan effectively untouched in all
of its dimensions, internal and external,

00:52:42.520 --> 00:52:44.850
short of formal nation state status?

00:52:44.850 --> 00:52:50.420
Perhaps that's the basis upon which we should
think about encouraging the Chinese and the

00:52:50.420 --> 00:52:55.280
Taiwanese, both of whom would hate the formula,
by the way, to get around a negotiating table

00:52:55.280 --> 00:52:57.369
to begin to look at other models.”

00:52:57.369 --> 00:53:01.440
Sure enough, it's hard to detect interest
in such an approach right now.

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:05.040
"The policy of this government is to safeguard
the status quo.

00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:06.460
The status quo that the Republic of China,
Taiwan and the People's Republic of China

00:53:06.460 --> 00:53:07.460
have no jurisdiction over each other.

00:53:07.460 --> 00:53:11.651
The status quo that Taiwan is already a democracy,
the status quo is that the Taiwanese people

00:53:11.651 --> 00:53:14.670
have a say over Taiwan's future.”

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:20.200
And yet Beijing has made it very clear that
it does not accept the status quo.

00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:25.059
"They just keep - wish to maintain the status
quo.

00:53:25.059 --> 00:53:30.880
And then this kind of status quo of separation
will last forever.

00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:35.190
So this is not the good wishes - or the wishes
of mainland China.

00:53:35.190 --> 00:53:39.599
We wish to become reunified, but we haven't
laid down timetable.

00:53:39.599 --> 00:53:46.170
So that is why we have to make sure to have
all options open.”

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:49.420
How long can this tension hold?

00:53:49.420 --> 00:53:52.740
China refusing to accept the way things are.

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:56.470
The US determined to stand in its way.

00:53:56.470 --> 00:53:59.140
Taiwan clinging on for dear life.

00:53:59.140 --> 00:54:04.210
All of them know what the stakes would be
if it does come to war.

00:54:04.210 --> 00:54:08.559
Kevin Rudd spelled them out in brutal detail.

00:54:08.559 --> 00:54:14.220
"A general war involving at least three or
four, four or five countries, including the

00:54:14.220 --> 00:54:19.270
three largest economies in the world, the
United States, China and Japan.

00:54:19.270 --> 00:54:25.990
Secondly, tens, if not hundreds of thousands
of Taiwanese civilians dead, because the bottom

00:54:25.990 --> 00:54:28.980
line is Taiwan would fight.

00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:36.099
And thirdly, unknown American and Chinese
combatants, assuming America became engaged.

00:54:36.099 --> 00:54:43.079
And finally, the crashing of the global economy,
because you would then be thrown into deep

00:54:43.079 --> 00:54:48.810
recession and probably depression as there
was a seize-up in all forms of international

00:54:48.810 --> 00:54:58.609
commerce and trade and financial markets involving
these three globally dominant economies.”

00:54:58.609 --> 00:55:05.619
And yet, when he spoke to us in Taipei, Joseph
Wu insisted that in the long run, he's optimistic.

00:55:05.619 --> 00:55:10.829
Although he treads carefully about what might
be his greatest hope.

00:55:10.829 --> 00:55:14.210
"This democracy is going to prevail in the
long run.

00:55:14.210 --> 00:55:19.640
On the contrary, if you look at China's political
system, it might look powerful and it might

00:55:19.640 --> 00:55:21.609
look threatening.

00:55:21.609 --> 00:55:25.750
But authoritarian regimes might be profoundly
weak.

00:55:25.750 --> 00:55:32.039
Look at their domestic issues that they have
to handle these days.

00:55:32.039 --> 00:55:36.770
Maybe all of a sudden the Chinese people will
say that, no, this is not what we want.

00:55:36.770 --> 00:55:37.940
We want something like Taiwan.”

00:55:37.940 --> 00:55:43.710
"So you're counting on the Chinese Communist
Party collapsing and democracy coming to China?

00:55:43.710 --> 00:55:45.170
Is that your greatest hope?”

00:55:45.170 --> 00:55:48.030
"No, that's not what I say and I'm not going
to predict.

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:51.230
But we have faith in Taiwan's democracy.

00:55:51.230 --> 00:55:53.460
As long as we are democratic.

00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:56.640
I think we will be doing fine.”

00:55:56.640 --> 00:56:00.789
It sounds like trusting in the tides of history.

00:56:00.789 --> 00:56:06.109
But in the dangerous years ahead, it will
be down to everyone to keep the peace.

